      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lisbon was named by John Moore after Lisbon, Portugal, a tribute that still echoes in the village’s name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisbon%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village joined Kendall County in 1841, marking its official entry into Illinois statehood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisbon%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A post office, schoolhouse, and early enterprise appeared together, signaling Lisbon’s first steps toward a self‑sufficient community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisbon%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today Lisbon hosts the Lisbon Consolidated School, a modern hub for local education that traces its roots back to the village’s original schoolhouse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisbon%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "John Moore Park offers a green escape for residents, while the nearby West Lisbon Church and Lisbon Bethlehem Lutheran Church reflect the village’s long‑standing religious heritage.",
